WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:02.853
BERNARD ARNAULT:
Are you familiar with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.853 --> 00:00:07.500
man named Bernard Arnault? He’s the richest man&nbsp;
in the world and the CEO and Chairman of luxury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.960
fashion brand Louis Vuitton Moet Hennessy.&nbsp;
On the outside, his company is iconic for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.840
their flamboyant purses and bags coming in at&nbsp;
thousands of dollars each. But, on the inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:25.200
there’s nothing flamboyant about the company&nbsp;
or Bernard whatsoever. This makes sense because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.960
Bernard didn’t found a single one of the dozens of&nbsp;
fashion brands that he owns today. He actually got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.400
his start in his family’s construction business&nbsp;
which gave him enough money to buyout the parent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.500
company of Christian Dior. From there, he&nbsp;
would engage in constant corporate raiding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:47.460
hostile takeovers, and brutal backstabbing to&nbsp;
build the empire that is LVMH today. They’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:54.240
bought out everyone from Tiffany and Bulgari to&nbsp;
Sephora and Hermes. In fact, here’s a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.940
all the brands that LVMH currently owns or&nbsp;
has owned in the past. With each and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.060
one of these brands, Bernard followed a very&nbsp;
simple formula. Buy a substantial predatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.380
stake in the business of your liking, promise&nbsp;
that you’re not looking to takeover the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.140
engineer internal strife amongst the existing&nbsp;
team, and finally make an offer that they can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.600
refuse. In the meantime, he’s also been keen to&nbsp;
ensure that he doesn’t get overthrown himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.420
One of the first things he did after taking over&nbsp;
LVMH was acquire enough shares to form a blocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.060
minority which means that no one can successfully&nbsp;
challenge his power over the company. While toxic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.700
this strategy has played out in Bernard’s favor&nbsp;
way more often than not. I mean, that’s why he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.340
the richest person in the world, but there&nbsp;
are a few notable exceptions as was the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.880
with Gucci. Bernard approached Gucci just like he&nbsp;
did with any other brand, but little did he know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.220
Gucci would put up the fight of their lives and&nbsp;
this takeover would end up being one of Bernard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:02.700
most public failures. So, here’s how Louis&nbsp;
Vuitton got humiliated trying to takeover Gucci.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.940
FRIENDLY FIRE:&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:16.380
Taking a look back, the bloody battle for Gucci&nbsp;
dates back to early 1999. The CEO of Gucci,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:22.380
Domenico De Sole, had just put in a stellar year&nbsp;
with Gucci. He had nearly doubled the stock price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:30.540
from $32 to $56 and their star designer, Tom Ford,&nbsp;
was making heads turn around the world. Gucci was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.400
no longer the struggling business desperate for a&nbsp;
bailout that they were just 5 years earlier when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.400
De Sole tookover. But, De Sole was just getting&nbsp;
started. Yes, he had successfully rescued the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.860
company from bankruptcy, but he wasn’t just&nbsp;
looking to save the company. What he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:53.220
do was make Gucci a fashion giant that was feared&nbsp;
and respected around the world. And last year’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.240
stock performance was exactly the momentum&nbsp;
that De Sole needed to make this evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:03.840
but there was one fear in the back of his&nbsp;
mind: Bernard Arnault. For the past year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.180
rumors had gone rampant that Bernard was&nbsp;
looking to takeover Gucci. On the outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:14.580
De Sole would put on a brave man facade that&nbsp;
he wasn’t worried Bernard or his predatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:21.720
hobby. But, internally, De Sole was petrified.&nbsp;
He had only been CEO for 5 years, but he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:28.440
been involved with gucci since the early 1980s,&nbsp;
and this was by far his life’s greatest work. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.960
you can bet that De Sole had no intention&nbsp;
of giving Gucci up to some French wolf just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.880
so that he could add it onto his endless list&nbsp;
of brands. As a result, De Sole was keeping a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.220
close pulse on the situation. He would regularly&nbsp;
call Gucci’s investment banker, Morgan Stanley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:50.040
and inquire if any outsized purchases of Gucci&nbsp;
stock had been made. In fact, he would make one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:57.540
such call on this fateful day: January 5, 1999.&nbsp;
But, something was different today as De Sole felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.820
that they were finally out of the woods. Instead&nbsp;
of inquiring about any large purchases, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.940
proudly tell the banker “it looks like we’re in&nbsp;
the clear now!” But, it was only hours later that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.080
he would get a frantic call from his assistant.&nbsp;
Louis Vuitton’s president, Yves Carcelle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:20.040
an old friend of De Sole, was on the line. But,&nbsp;
almost instantaneously, De Sole could tell that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.200
Carcelle wasn’t calling to get drinks or discuss&nbsp;
the fashion industry, Carcelle was calling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.020
inform. He was calling to inform that LVMH had&nbsp;
acquired a 5% stake in Gucci and that the news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.540
was gonna go public later that day. He went onto&nbsp;
assure De Sole that Bernard’s purchase was nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:42.300
but passive and that he wanted the two brands to&nbsp;
work together. But, knowing Bernard’s history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:48.600
De Sole knew that this was complete and utter&nbsp;
BS. He would hang up the phone and rush into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.460
the London Gucci office calling everyone he&nbsp;
could think of. He called Morgan Stanley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.040
he called their anti-takeover specialist,&nbsp;
and then his heart sunk. The anti-takeover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.380
specialist that they had been working with&nbsp;
forever just happened to have a 1 year long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:10.920
sabbatical starting in just 1 week. At this point,&nbsp;
De Sole lost it. He hung up the phone and hollered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:17.280
“If Arnault wants the company, he can have it!&nbsp;
I don’t give a blank. I’ll just go sailing!”

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.500
HOPELESS DEFENSE:&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:30.240
Though De Sole had a little outburst there, he by&nbsp;
no means meant what he said. Though he did love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.360
sailing, he wasn’t gonna sit back and let this&nbsp;
French tycoon bully Gucci. So, he would call back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.060
Morgan Stanley and ask to get in contact with the&nbsp;
boss of his former anti takeover specialist. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.860
got him in contact with a man named Michael&nbsp;
Zaoui who was the head of Morgan Stanley’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.780
European merger-advisory group. Normally, such&nbsp;
a defense would have been under his pay grade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:58.020
but Zaoui was more than happy to help because he&nbsp;
had his own vendetta against Arnault. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.060
he had previously represented the Guinness Brewery&nbsp;
in their own fight against LVMH just a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:10.200
ago. And that fight didn’t go as well as Zaoui had&nbsp;
hoped, so this was his chance to get revenge. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.580
would come over to the Gucci office in a matter&nbsp;
of minutes bringing with him Morgan Stanley’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:20.820
defense. But, when they opened up the file, they&nbsp;
would be met with another bombshell. It turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:26.220
out that the anti-takeover specialist wasn’t&nbsp;
just going on a sabbatical but he hadn’t done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:33.480
jack in terms of creating a defense. In fact, the&nbsp;
entire defense consisted of just two clauses. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:39.480
De Sole was allowed to resign from the company in&nbsp;
the event of a hostile takeover. And two, Tom Ford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:46.140
was also allowed to resign if someone acquired&nbsp;
more than 35%. Zaoui couldn’t believe it. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:51.960
call up his employee and yell “Where is the fing&nbsp;
defense? What have you guys been doing all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.620
time?” To this, the employee didn’t really have a&nbsp;
response. It wouldn’t be surprising if this guy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:05.580
sabbatical turned into a permanent one, but that&nbsp;
didn’t really help Gucci’s case. As Gucci’s CFO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:12.240
observed, “You guys are toast.” For Gucci, there&nbsp;
was really only way out of this. They would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.180
to find someone else to buy a large enough stake&nbsp;
in Gucci before LVMH tookover. This way, LVMH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:23.880
wouldn’t be able to acquire a majority, but this&nbsp;
was much easier said than down. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:30.780
Gucci was worth $8.7 billion and while this was&nbsp;
cheap money for Bernard, for everyone else in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.940
fashion industry, this was serious business and&nbsp;
they couldn’t just make an investment decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:43.080
overnight. So, De Sole only had one option,&nbsp;
try his best to stall Bernard, but Bernard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:48.840
had no intention of moving slow. Just a few days&nbsp;
after his initial purchase, LVMH would acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:56.460
another 9.5% in Gucci. And within 10 days, Bernard&nbsp;
would own over a quarter of the company. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.340
meantime, De Sole was desperately calling every&nbsp;
CEO in the fashion industry pleading for them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:08.580
invest in Gucci or at the very least not sell&nbsp;
off any stake that they may already have. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:15.960
call after call, De Sole was politely refused. For&nbsp;
all the other CEOs, making Bernard into an enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.180
was simply a road they didn’t want to go down.&nbsp;
Given that no one else wanted to help Gucci,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:27.060
De Sole knew that there was only one more person&nbsp;
to ask: Bernard himself. He would setup a meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:31.800
with Bernard at Morgan Stanley and request&nbsp;
that he slow down his buying and let Gucci&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:39.360
stay independent. Bernard was open to the request,&nbsp;
but in return he wanted 3 full seats on the Gucci&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:45.000
board. It became clear to De Sole right then an&nbsp;
there that Bernard wasn’t here for a compromise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:51.120
He was just looking to grow his control in Gucci&nbsp;
through another avenue. De Sole would of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:58.620
reject this offer, but this simply led to Bernard&nbsp;
upping his stake to 34.4%. Bernard was taunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:05.820
Gucci. Just another 0.6% and Tom Ford could leave&nbsp;
with no repercussions. Despite Bernard’s charming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:12.600
personality, this was no friendly deal. This&nbsp;
was war and it was now do or die for Gucci.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.120
FIGHT FIRE WITH FIRE:
De&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:25.980
Sole was done begging everyone in the fashion&nbsp;
industry. Even if someone was willing to help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:32.580
Bernard would probably just destroy them as well.&nbsp;
So, what Gucci had to do was fight fire with fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:37.920
and after consulting with a slew of lawyers,&nbsp;
he knew exactly what he had to do. He was gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:44.040
dilute LVMH into oblivion. You know how&nbsp;
companies like to buyback their shares to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.440
increase their stock price. Well, they can do&nbsp;
the opposite too and issue a bunch of shares.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.780
For obvious reasons companies try to avoid doing&nbsp;
this unless they really to need to raise money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.780 --> 00:10:01.620
Gucci didn’t need money, but they could issue a&nbsp;
crap ton of shares to dilute Bernard. Bernard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:06.840
on the other hand was well aware of this strategy,&nbsp;
but he thought he was protected. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.460
American regulation specifies that companies&nbsp;
can’t dilute more than 20% of their company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:18.060
without a special waiver. This is obviously to&nbsp;
protect shareholders from over dilution. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.900
Bernard figured, worst case scenario,&nbsp;
if Gucci tried to pull this stunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:28.260
he’d have to pay another 20% for the company, no&nbsp;
big deal. Also, there was no way Gucci would get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:33.900
a waiver just to stop an acquisition. Waivers&nbsp;
are reserved for truly dire situations as in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:40.260
this company will go bankrupt if they don’t dilute&nbsp;
more. Gucci was obviously not in that position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:46.380
so Bernard was quite confident in his own&nbsp;
position. But, it turns out that Bernard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:53.940
lawyers had missed some fine print. You see, that&nbsp;
20% rule only applied to American companies. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:59.700
technically, Gucci was legally allowed to dilute&nbsp;
themselves into oblivion. Of course, this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:04.980
hurt all Gucci shareholders, but given that LVMH&nbsp;
was the biggest one, they would be hurt the most.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:11.700
And with that De Sole started printing shares&nbsp;
left and right. Little to say, Bernard was truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:17.460
pissed and he would decide to leverage some fine&nbsp;
print himself. According to Gucci’s own bylaws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:22.680
anyone with a stake larger than 10% could request&nbsp;
a special shareholder meeting. At this meeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:28.080
Bernard wanted to nominate a new person to&nbsp;
the Gucci board and essentially stage a coup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:34.260
Things were getting heated and De Sole needed a&nbsp;
power play to get the upper hand. While printing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:39.000
shares could stall, it didn’t solve the issue of&nbsp;
not having a large stakeholder that could block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:45.000
out LVMH. Gucci’s resilience and willingness&nbsp;
to fight LVMH did pique the interest of more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:50.760
potential buyers, but no one was willing to&nbsp;
pull the trigger yet. And De Sole didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:57.480
time to burn so he decided, if I can’t find&nbsp;
a shareholder I’ll create one. I’ll just give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:02.940
all the shares to Gucci employees through a&nbsp;
massive Employee Stock Ownership Plan or ESOP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:10.020
And that’s exactly what he did. In late Febraury&nbsp;
of 1999, Gucci issued 20 million shares to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:16.620
employees which effectively diluted Bernard&nbsp;
from 35% down to 25%. For the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:22.200
Bernard could feel Gucci slipping from&nbsp;
his grips but the fight was far from over.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.000
THE FINAL BATTLE:&nbsp;

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:34.500
This brilliant move from Gucci turned heads&nbsp;
not just within the fashion industry but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:40.200
business world as a whole. But, while this was&nbsp;
a great victory for Gucci, De Sole didn’t let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:44.760
this distract him from his end goal which was&nbsp;
to escape the clutches of Bernard by finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:49.620
an investor. And Gucci’s ability to hold their&nbsp;
own against one of the most ruthless businessmen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:55.740
in the world gave potential investors much more&nbsp;
confidence. And this is when a man named Francois&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:02.460
Pinault would enter the scene. De Sole admits that&nbsp;
he had no clue who Pinault was, but not wanting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:08.280
ignore any leads, De Sole agreed to meet up with&nbsp;
Pinault in early March. It turns out that Pinault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:13.380
was one France’s wealthiest business men and owned&nbsp;
classics like Christie’s auction house, Converse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:18.480
shoes, and Samsonite luggage. At the meeting&nbsp;
De Sole would give his classic speech about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:23.520
how he and Tom Ford saved Gucci from bankruptcy&nbsp;
and how they were trying to now save Gucci from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.920
yet another demon. Pinault was impressed with&nbsp;
the first meeting itself, but after a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:35.400
meeting that included Tom Ford, Pinault was sold.&nbsp;
He too wanted to move fast. In fact, he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:42.240
move even faster than Gucci as he didn’t want to&nbsp;
lose the company to LVMH. So, he gave Gucci an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:49.740
ultimatum. Finish the deal in a week or I’m out.&nbsp;
De Sole still had an inkling of doubt if this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:55.440
the right choice, but Tom Ford was confident that&nbsp;
this was the way forward which gave De Sole the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.840
confidence to agree to a multi billion dollar deal&nbsp;
in a matter of hours. By that evening, De Sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:05.880
would put together a confidential team of&nbsp;
investment bankers and lawyers all working to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:11.040
complete this deal. Speaking of the deal, the deal&nbsp;
was that De Sole would issue another 40 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.800
shares of stock which Pinault would purchase&nbsp;
for $75 per share. This would cost a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:22.860
$3 billion and give Pinault a 42% stake in Gucci&nbsp;
while simultaneously diluting Bernard down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:29.640
19.6%. This would essentially block out Bernard&nbsp;
from being able to acquire a majority stake. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:34.980
the deal didn’t stop right there either. You&nbsp;
see, Pinault had another ace up his sleeve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:41.400
He suggested buying YSL and letting De Sole and&nbsp;
Tom run that company as well. This itself was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:47.040
extremely generous offer but what made it even&nbsp;
more satisfying was that Bernard had just passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:53.340
up on buying YSL a few months ago so that he could&nbsp;
focus on getting Gucci. And now, Bernard was gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.340 --> 00:15:00.300
lose on out Gucci and YSL. De Sole and Tom would&nbsp;
of course take Pinault up on this offer and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:05.520
the blink of an eye, Gucci would go from trying&nbsp;
to escape Louis Vuitton’s clutches to insulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:11.040
Louis Vuitton. Bernard would of course throw&nbsp;
a tantrum. He would sue to block this deal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:18.060
offer to buy the entire company for $8.7 billion.&nbsp;
But now, Gucci had the upperhand, and they weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:24.480
gonna tolerate any more of Bernard’s antics. And&nbsp;
eventually, Bernard had no choice but to keep his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:31.200
minority stake or just sell it off and move on and&nbsp;
that’s exactly what he finally do in late 2001.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.180
THE AFTERMATH:&nbsp;

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:43.620
In the end, Bernard’s hostility actually ended&nbsp;
up working in Gucci’s favor. Not only were they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:48.600
able to escape LVMH, but they were able to find&nbsp;
someone that was willing to back them and truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:55.200
form a mutually beneficial partnership. And let’s&nbsp;
just say Pinault made an awesome deal as Gucci now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:01.620
accounts of 60% of his company’s revenue and 70%&nbsp;
of his company’s profits. It ended up working out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:07.200
just fine for Bernard as well as he is now the&nbsp;
world’s richest man. But, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:12.120
